    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=2$.\n\nRearrange as equal sums of radicals, then square twice to obtain $3(x-2)^2=0$.",
      "worked": "The domain is $x\\geq\\frac{3}{7}$. Rearrange:\n$$\\sqrt{7x-3}+\\sqrt{3x+5}=\\sqrt{6x-1}+\\sqrt{4x+3}.$$\nSquaring both sides gives\n$$\\begin{align*}10x+2+2\\sqrt{(7x-3)(3x+5)}&=10x+2+2\\sqrt{(6x-1)(4x+3)}\\\\\\sqrt{(7x-3)(3x+5)}&=\\sqrt{(6x-1)(4x+3)}.\\end{align*}$$\nSquare again:\n$$\\begin{align*}(7x-3)(3x+5)&=(6x-1)(4x+3)\\\\21x^2+26x-15&=24x^2+14x-3\\\\3x^2-12x+12&=0\\\\3(x-2)^2&=0\\\\x&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nAt $x=2$, all four radicals equal $\\sqrt{11}$, so the original equation reads $0=0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
